<header class="hero"><div class="in">
<span class="tag">Public Service Announcement</span>
<h1>One pill<br><span>can kill.</span></h1>
<p class="lead">That “Xanax,” “Percocet,” or “Adderall” from a friend or an app might be fentanyl. You can’t see it, smell it, or taste it.</p>
<a class="btn" href="#facts">See the facts</a>
</div></header>

<section id="facts"><div class="in">
<h2>The facts</h2><p class="kick">Teens are the ones getting hurt, and most never meant to use fentanyl.</p>
<div class="grid g3" id="stats">
<div class="stat"><div class="n"><span data-to="182">0</span>%</div><p>rise in fentanyl-involved overdose deaths, ages 10–19, 2019 to 2021</p><small>CDC MMWR [1]</small></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="n">1 in 4</div><p>teen overdose deaths showed evidence of counterfeit pills (24.5%)</p><small>CDC MMWR [1]</small></div>
<div class="stat"><div class="n">2 in 3</div><p>teen overdose deaths happened with someone nearby who could have helped</p><small>CDC researcher via NPR [2]</small></div>
</div>
<div class="alert">Fentanyl slows and can stop your breathing. Pill-to-pill doses vary, so one can be fine and the next fatal.</div>
</div></section>

<section class="alt" id="spot"><div class="in">
<h2>Spot the fake</h2><p class="kick">Tap the pill you think is dangerous.</p>
<div class="pills">
<button class="pillbtn" data-r="Pharmacy pill. Made, tested, and dosed. Safe only if it was prescribed to you."><div class="dot">M30</div><b>Pill A</b></button>
<button class="pillbtn" data-r="Fake pill. Looks identical, but the dose is a guess."><div class="dot">M30</div><b>Pill B</b></button>
</div>
<div class="res" id="pillres" role="status"></div>
<p style="margin-top:14px"><b>Trick question:</b> you can’t tell by looking. Neither can a dealer, a friend, or an app. Only a pharmacy pill prescribed to you is known to be safe.</p>
</div></section>

<section id="danger"><div class="in">
<h2>Where it hides</h2><p class="kick">Three ways teens end up with fentanyl by accident.</p>
<div class="grid g3">
<div class="box"><b>Social media deals</b><p>Dealers sell fake pills through apps teens use. The CDC says social media makes them easy to buy [1].</p></div>
<div class="box"><b>“Just one to focus or sleep”</b><p>Most teens who take a fake pill thought it was a normal prescription.</p></div>
<div class="box"><b>Mixing</b><p>Fentanyl plus alcohol or other depressants raises overdose risk.</p></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="alt" id="quiz"><div class="in">
<h2>What would you do?</h2><p class="kick">Three quick scenarios.</p>
<div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div>
<div class="q" id="q"></div>
</div></section>

<section id="help"><div class="in">
<h2>How to stay safe</h2><p class="kick">Five things that can save a life.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><b>Only take medicine prescribed to you</b> and filled at a pharmacy.</li>
<li><b>Never buy pills online or from a friend.</b> “It’s legit” is not proof.</li>
<li><b>Carry naloxone (Narcan).</b> It reverses opioid overdoses and is sold over the counter.</li>
<li><b>Know the signs:</b> won’t wake up, slow or no breathing, blue or gray lips, pinpoint pupils.</li>
<li><b>Act fast:</b> call 911, give naloxone, stay with the person.</li>
</ol>
<div class="alert">Need help or someone to talk to? Call or text <b>988</b>, or call SAMHSA’s free, confidential 24/7 helpline at <b>1-800-662-HELP (4357)</b>.</div>
</div></section>

